//! The Basic module: the bottom of the Jairs standard library.
//!
//! This is written in Jairs, not in the compiler (PLAN.md decision #5). That is
//! what forces `#foreign` into the first slice: the bottom of a standard library
//! is a syscall, and there is no way to express it otherwise.
//!
//! Everything here must be writable in the Jairs-0 subset. That constraint is
//! real and it bites -- see the note on integer printing at the end of this file.

libc :: #system_library "c";

/// POSIX write(2): writes `count` bytes from `buf` to the file descriptor `fd`.
///
/// Jairs strings are `{data: *u8, count: s64}` and are NOT NUL-terminated (ADR-0004),
/// which is exactly the shape `write` wants: a pointer and a length. No conversion, no
/// temporary storage, no `to_c_string`.
write :: (fd: s64, buf: *u8, count: s64) -> s64 #foreign libc "write";

/// Terminates the process with `status`.
///
/// Declared here so programs have a way out that does not depend on `main` returning,
/// which the slice does not model yet.
exit :: (status: s64) #foreign libc "exit";

/// Allocates `size` bytes and returns a pointer to them, or `null` on failure (ADR-0060 §2).
///
/// The honest bottom of a standard library until wave W7 replaces it: libc's own `malloc`. A
/// `#foreign` pointer return is an ordinary register scalar (ADR-0051), so this needs no new
/// machinery. It cannot be called at *compile time* — ADR-0006 gates comptime FFI, and a host
/// pointer read through the VM's own address space would be a plausible wrong value.
malloc :: (size: s64) -> *u8 #foreign libc "malloc";

/// Releases memory obtained from [`malloc`]. Passing `null` is defined (a no-op), which is why a
/// program can `free` unconditionally.
free :: (p: *u8) #foreign libc "free";

/// The size of the temporary-storage arena, in bytes (ADR-0065 §2).
///
/// Fixed rather than growable: a growable arena would either move on `realloc` — invalidating every
/// pointer `talloc` already returned — or need a block list, which is more than the slice wants. 64
/// KiB is enough for the scratch uses here; a later wave can make it a context field.
TEMP_REGION_SIZE :: 65536;

/// Allocates `n` bytes from the per-context temporary-storage arena (ADR-0065).
///
/// The bytes stay valid until the next [`reset_temporary_storage`]; there is no per-piece free. This
/// is the allocator for scratch space a computation throws away wholesale — building a string to
/// print, say — where freeing each piece would be noise.
///
/// The arena is `context.temp_data`, a region lazily `malloc`'d on first use (it is null in a fresh
/// context, ADR-0057 §5), and `context.temp_mark` is the bump cursor — a *byte count*, so the next
/// allocation is at `temp_data + temp_mark`. Returns `null` on a failed `malloc` or when the region is
/// full, matching [`malloc`]'s convention (ADR-0065 §3): running out of scratch is a resource
/// condition a caller may handle, not a bug that should trap.
///
/// It reads the context, so it travels with the call: a callee's `talloc` uses its caller's arena.
talloc :: (n: s64) -> *u8 {
    if context.temp_data == null {
        context.temp_data = malloc(TEMP_REGION_SIZE);
        if context.temp_data == null {
            return null;
        }
    }
    if context.temp_mark + n > TEMP_REGION_SIZE {
        return null;
    }
    // `*u8 + s64` is byte arithmetic (element-scaled on a `*u8`, which is what a byte arena wants,
    // ADR-0064 §2), so this is the address `temp_mark` bytes into the region.
    p := context.temp_data + context.temp_mark;
    context.temp_mark = context.temp_mark + n;
    return p;
}

/// Rewinds the temporary-storage cursor, freeing everything [`talloc`] handed out at once (ADR-0065).
///
/// It does **not** release the region — the `malloc`'d 64 KiB stays for the program's life, because
/// reuse is the point of an arena. It rewinds `context.temp_mark` to 0, so the next `talloc` hands out
/// from the start again. A pointer from before the reset is dangling afterwards, exactly as it would
/// be after a `free` — the arena's contract is "valid until the next reset".
reset_temporary_storage :: () {
    context.temp_mark = 0;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Runtime type information (ADR-0075)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `type_info(T)` returns a `*Type_Info` describing `T`. The struct is declared **here, in Jairs**,
// rather than inside the compiler, and that is a deliberate decision with a cost.
//
// The reason is that a `Type_Info` must be *spellable*: a program that reflects has to be able to write
// `info: *Type_Info` and read `info.name`. No compiler-declared type is spellable — `t: Type;` and
// `c: Context;` both report E0212, because `Context`'s fields are a Rust `const` and a compiler-declared
// type has no `DeclId` for name resolution to find (ADR-0057 §1). Declaring the struct in Jairs makes it
// spellable for free: it is then an ordinary nominal declaration, and field access, layout, pointers and
// `using` all apply with no new case in the compiler.
//
// The cost is that the compiler now depends on a declaration it does not own. That dependency is
// **validated** rather than trusted: the compiler looks `Type_Info` up by name and checks its field
// names, types and order, refusing with E0265 if they do not match what it expects. Editing this struct
// therefore produces a diagnostic naming the mismatch, not a silent read of whatever is at offset 8 —
// which matters because a wrong offset is this project's named failure mode and a refusal is not.
//
// Keep the fields, their types and their order in step with `jr-sema`'s `TYPE_INFO_FIELDS`.

/// What kind of type a [`Type_Info`] describes.
///
/// An enum rather than an integer so that a `switch` over it is exhaustiveness-checked (ADR-0067),
/// which is the whole reason to have a tag at all. The members are the shapes the language has;
/// `#run`-only shapes such as the type of a type never reach here, because `type_info` refuses a type
/// with no runtime layout (E0266).
Type_Info_Kind :: enum {
    VOID;
    BOOL;
    INTEGER;
    FLOAT;
    STRING;
    POINTER;
    ARRAY;
    VIEW;
    STRUCT;
    UNION;
    VARIANT;
    ENUM;
    PROCEDURE;
    /// A `[..]T` dynamic array (ADR-0136, member added ADR-0140). Distinct from `VIEW`: a dynamic
    /// array owns its heap storage and carries a `capacity`, so a program reflecting on one must be
    /// able to tell the two apart — before this member `type_info([..]T)` was a hard const-eval
    /// error, because the compiler names the shape and found no member to bind. **Appended** rather
    /// than placed beside `VIEW` so no existing member's value moves: the compiler reads this member
    /// by name (`jr-db`'s `type_info_kind_name`), but a snapshot or a caller comparing raw ordinals
    /// would see every later shape renumber if it were inserted mid-list.
    DYNAMIC_ARRAY;
}

/// Which operating system the program is being compiled for (ADR-0180 §1).
///
/// The type of the `os()` intrinsic, which sema folds to a constant of this enum before any back end
/// sees a call. Declared **here**, in Jairs, rather than inside the compiler for exactly the reason
/// `Type_Info` is (ADR-0075 §2): a caller has to be able to *name* the type to store the value, and a
/// type the compiler owns and no source file can spell would be unusable.
///
/// An enum rather than an integer so a `switch` over it is exhaustiveness-checked (ADR-0067) — which is
/// what makes "this program does not handle Windows" a compile error instead of a silently wrong branch.
///
/// **Three members.** A fourth host is a *library* edit plus one arm in the compiler's mapping, which is
/// the whole point of the declaration living here. Nothing reads these by ordinal: the compiler resolves
/// the member it wants **by name**, the way it resolves a `Type_Info_Kind`, so a member added in the
/// middle would renumber the others harmlessly.
Operating_System :: enum {
    MACOS;
    LINUX;
    WINDOWS;
}

/// What the compiler knows about a type at run time (ADR-0075 §3, ADR-0077).
///
/// Five fields since ADR-0077 added `id`; `name` is the one that made this wave need ADR-0075 §1 first: a compile-time
/// aggregate holding a string used to be refused, so a `Type_Info` could not carry a name.
///
/// Deliberately **without per-kind detail** — a struct's field list, an array's element type, a
/// procedure's signature. Each of those is a variable-length member, so it wants a view or a
/// pointer-and-count, and that raises a memory-ownership question (static data the back end emits,
/// versus a comptime-built table) which is its own decision. `size` and `alignment` are enough to be
/// useful — they are what `size_of` would give, which this language does not otherwise have — and the
/// shape extends by adding fields, which does not break a reader that names only these four.
/// One declaration a metaprogram found by note, as `noted_declarations` reports it (ADR-0153 §1).
///
/// This is W6's headline claim made real: a metaprogram can *iterate* the declarations carrying a note,
/// in an ordinary `for`, instead of unrolling `noted_name(tag, 0)`, `noted_name(tag, 1)`, … to a bound it
/// guessed. ADR-0100 §2 stated that limit honestly and named what it was waiting for — a compiler-emitted
/// table — which ADR-0152 then built.
Declaration :: struct {
    /// The declared name, as written.
    name: string;
    /// The note's value — `@requires "x"` gives `"x"` — or `""` for a bare note like `@deprecated`.
    ///
    /// `""` rather than an absent marker, because a bare note genuinely has no value and a script that
    /// wants to know whether one was written can compare against `""` without a second field.
    note_value: string;
}

/// One field of a struct, union or variant, as reflection reports it (ADR-0152 §3).
///
/// This is the **variable-length** member ADR-0078 §3 deferred, and what it was waiting for was a way
/// for the compiler to emit an array and hand out a view of it: a `Type_Info` is an interned value, and
/// a view inside an interned value used to mean interning a *pointer* — which ADR-0074 found gives the
/// evaluator's own address. `fields` is a view over a compiler-emitted read-only table instead, so no
/// address is ever interned.
Type_Info_Field :: struct {
    /// The field's source name.
    name: string;
    /// The field's type, as a type id — the same kind of id `Type_Info.id` carries, so a program
    /// compares it against another type's `id`.
    ///
    /// An id rather than a `*Type_Info`, for the reason `element` is one: a pointer to the field's own
    /// info would need that info built and stored somewhere, and an id needs nothing built.
    ty: s64;
    /// The field's byte offset from the start of the aggregate, in the target's layout.
    offset: s64;
}

Type_Info :: struct {
    /// The type's canonical identity — its pool id, widened to `s64` (ADR-0077).
    ///
    /// An opaque token, not a number to do arithmetic on: two `type_info(T)` calls carry the same `id`
    /// while two distinct types never do, which is what lets `any_as` check a value's type without
    /// comparing pointers (two `type_info(T)` calls live in different slots, so their addresses differ).
    id: s64;
    /// Which shape this type is.
    kind: Type_Info_Kind;
    /// The type's source name, or a builtin's spelling (`"s64"`).
    name: string;
    /// The type's runtime size in bytes.
    size: s64;
    /// The type's runtime alignment in bytes.
    alignment: s64;
    /// A struct's field count, or an array's length; 0 for a kind that has neither (ADR-0078 §1).
    ///
    /// One field for both because both answer "how many"; which it means is read from `kind`, exactly as
    /// `element` is. 0 is a real answer — a scalar has no count — not a sentinel for "unknown".
    count: s64;
    /// An array's element or a pointer's pointee, as a type id; 0 for a kind with no element (ADR-0078 §1).
    ///
    /// The same kind of id as [`Type_Info.id`], so a program compares it against another type's `id` to
    /// ask "is this a `[]s64`?". It is an id rather than a `*Type_Info` because a pointer to the element's
    /// info would need that info built and stored somewhere — deferred; an id needs nothing built.
    element: s64;
    /// Every field of a struct, union or variant, in declaration order (ADR-0152 §3).
    ///
    /// Empty for every other kind — a scalar has no fields, and that is a real answer rather than a
    /// sentinel. The view points into a **read-only table the compiler emitted**, so it is valid for the
    /// life of the program and must not be written through.
    fields: []Type_Info_Field;
    /// Whether an integer type is signed (ADR-0189 §3).
    ///
    /// Meaningful only where `kind` is `INTEGER`, and `false` for every other kind — which is a real
    /// answer rather than a sentinel, since nothing else has a sign.
    ///
    /// **This is what makes printing an integer correct.** Width comes from `size`, and without this a
    /// caller had to read the first byte of `name` and hope it was `s` or `u`. That works today by
    /// accident: a builtin cannot be aliased at file scope, so an integer's name is always one of
    /// `s8`..`u64`. Relying on it is how a `u64` above 2^63 comes to print as negative the day aliasing
    /// starts working.
    ///
    /// Appended last, so no existing member's offset moves — the discipline `Type_Info_Kind`'s
    /// `DYNAMIC_ARRAY` member records at length.
    signed: bool;
}

/// A value whose type is carried alongside it (ADR-0076 §3).
///
/// `Any` is how a procedure takes an argument whose type it does not know: the `type` says what the
/// value is and `data` points at it. `any_of(*x)` builds one and `any_as(a, T)` reads it back, trapping
/// if the type does not match — the check is the whole point of carrying the type, and a read that
/// ignored it would make this a `void*` with extra steps.
///
/// Declared here rather than inside the compiler for the reason [`Type_Info`] is: a program has to be
/// able to *name* it — `takes :: (a: Any)` is the entire use case — and no compiler-declared type is
/// spellable. The compiler validates these fields on lookup, so editing them is a diagnostic rather
/// than a wrong read.
///
/// **`data` is a `*u8` and nothing is reinterpreted.** A pointer's layout does not depend on what it
/// points at, so erasing `*Point` to `*u8` loses no bits and emits no code — it is a statement in the
/// type system only. That is why the conversion is allowed *here* and a general pointer cast is not
/// (ADR-0076 §1): a general one would make a wrong pointee type a silent wrong read.
Any :: struct {
    /// What `data` points at.
    type: *Type_Info;
    /// The value itself, erased. Read it back with `any_as` rather than by casting.
    data: *u8;
}

/// The standard output file descriptor.
STDOUT :: 1;
/// The standard error file descriptor.
STDERR :: 2;

/// Writes `fmt` to standard output, replacing each `%` with the next argument (ADR-0189 §4).
///
/// ```jairs
/// print("x = %, ok = %\n", 42, true);        // x = 42, ok = true
/// print("100%% sure\n");                     // 100% sure
/// print("%\n", some_point);                  // {x = 3, y = 4}
/// ```
///
/// Returns the number of bytes written.
///
/// # Why `%` and not `{}` or `printf`
///
/// It is Jai's placeholder, and this library imitates Jai. It also needs no width, precision or type
/// letter: the *argument* carries its type, through `Any` and `Type_Info`, so `%` never disagrees with
/// what follows it. That is the whole reason a `printf`-shaped `%d` is wrong here — a `%d` handed a
/// float is undefined behaviour in C and unrepresentable here.
///
/// `%%` is a literal `%`. That is the only escape.
///
/// # What a wrong argument count does
///
/// Neither is an error, because a `print` that refuses to print is worse than one that tells you:
///
///   * too few arguments — the `%` renders as `%!(MISSING)`;
///   * too many — the extras are appended as `%!(EXTRA a, b)`.
///
/// Both are visible in the output and neither can be missed, which is what a diagnostic in a procedure
/// that cannot fail looks like.
///
/// # Buffering, and the one caveat
///
/// Output goes through a module-level buffer and reaches `write` once per call rather than once per
/// byte — the old `print_int` cost one syscall *per digit*. The buffer is a file-scope global, which is
/// where Jai keeps its equivalent and which ADR-0186 made possible.
///
/// **It is therefore not thread-safe**, and that is stated rather than hidden: two threads printing at
/// once interleave inside one buffer. Jai's own `print` uses the context's temporary storage and so is
/// per-thread; matching that needs `#add_context`, which ADR-0186 records as owed. Until then, a
/// threaded program prints from one thread.
print :: (fmt: string, args: ..Any) -> s64 {
    out_reset(STDOUT);
    format_into_out(fmt, args);
    return out_flush();
}

/// `print`, to standard error.
///
/// A separate procedure rather than a `to_standard_error := false` parameter, which is what Jai's takes.
/// The reason is Jairs' own: a defaulted parameter *after* a variadic cannot be reached, because every
/// trailing argument is packed into the variadic (ADR-0139 §1). So the choice is a second name or a
/// parameter before the format string, and a second name reads better at every call site.
print_error :: (fmt: string, args: ..Any) -> s64 {
    out_reset(STDERR);
    format_into_out(fmt, args);
    return out_flush();
}

/// `print`, with a newline appended.
///
/// Takes a format string and arguments like `print` does, so `print_line("x = %", x)` works. It used to
/// take a bare `string`; every existing call still compiles, because a call with no trailing arguments
/// packs an empty variadic and a format string with no `%` is copied through.
print_line :: (fmt: string, args: ..Any) -> s64 {
    out_reset(STDOUT);
    format_into_out(fmt, args);
    out_byte(cast(u8, NEWLINE));
    return out_flush();
}

/// Formats into a caller's buffer instead of writing, returning the bytes used (ADR-0189 §4).
///
/// The building block a caller needs when the destination is not a file descriptor — a network write, a
/// log record, a string to compare in a test. `buffer` is filled from the start and the result is how
/// many bytes were written; output longer than the buffer is **truncated**, and the return value says so
/// by equalling `buffer.count`.
///
/// Truncation rather than a failure flag, because the alternative is worse in both directions: a caller
/// that must handle an error for a debug line will ignore it, and growing the buffer needs an allocator
/// this procedure deliberately does not take.
format :: (buffer: []u8, fmt: string, args: ..Any) -> s64 {
    out_reset(NO_FD);
    format_into_out(fmt, args);
    n := out_count;
    if n > buffer.count {
        n = buffer.count;
    }
    i := 0;
    while i < n {
        buffer[i] = out_bytes[i];
        i = i + 1;
    }
    out_count = 0;
    return n;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The formatter (ADR-0189 §4)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

#scope_module

/// A file descriptor meaning "do not write" — `format` fills a caller's buffer instead.
///
/// `-1` rather than `0`, because 0 is standard input and a stray write to it would be a real bug rather
/// than a no-op.
NO_FD :: -1;

/// How many bytes accumulate before the buffer is flushed to its descriptor.
///
/// 4096 is one page and comfortably more than any single line. It bounds the *buffer*, not the output:
/// `out_byte` flushes when full, so a longer `print` costs more than one `write` and still completes.
OUT_CAPACITY :: 4096;

/// The output buffer.
///
/// A file-scope global, which ADR-0186 made possible and which is where Jai keeps its equivalent. Before
/// globals existed this would have had to be a caller-owned struct threaded through every call, and
/// `print("hi")` would have taken two arguments.
out_bytes: [OUT_CAPACITY]u8 = ---;

/// How many bytes of `out_bytes` are live.
out_count: s64 = 0;

/// Where a flush goes, or `NO_FD` to accumulate only.
out_fd: s64 = NO_FD;

/// How many bytes this `print` has produced, flushes included.
out_written: s64 = 0;

/// ASCII codes, named because a bare `48` in the middle of digit arithmetic reads as a magic number.
ZERO_DIGIT :: 48;
/// `-`
MINUS :: 45;
/// `%`
PERCENT :: 37;
/// `.`
DOT :: 46;
/// `\n`
NEWLINE :: 10;
/// `a`, the first hex letter, so `0xff` rather than `0xFF`.
LOWER_A :: 97;

/// Starts a fresh formatting run at `fd`.
out_reset :: (fd: s64) {
    out_count = 0;
    out_fd = fd;
    out_written = 0;
}

/// Writes the buffer out and returns the total bytes this run produced.
out_flush :: () -> s64 {
    if out_count > 0 && out_fd != NO_FD {
        write(out_fd, *out_bytes[0], out_count);
        out_count = 0;
    }
    return out_written;
}

/// Appends one byte, flushing first when the buffer is full.
out_byte :: (b: u8) {
    if out_count >= OUT_CAPACITY {
        if out_fd == NO_FD {
            // Nowhere to flush to, so a `format` longer than the buffer stops here. The caller learns
            // this from a return value equal to their buffer's size, which is the documented contract.
            return;
        }
        write(out_fd, *out_bytes[0], out_count);
        out_count = 0;
    }
    out_bytes[out_count] = b;
    out_count = out_count + 1;
    out_written = out_written + 1;
}

/// Appends every byte of a string.
out_str :: (s: string) {
    i := 0;
    while i < s.count {
        out_byte((s.data + i).*);
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Numbers
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Appends an unsigned integer in decimal.
///
/// Digits are generated least-significant first into a 20-byte scratch array and emitted in reverse. 20
/// is exact: `u64`'s maximum is 18446744073709551615, which is 20 digits. The `print_digits` this replaced
/// recursed instead, one frame per digit, because arrays did not exist when it was written — the note at
/// the bottom of this file records that arc.
out_u64 :: (v: u64) {
    if v == 0 {
        out_byte(cast(u8, ZERO_DIGIT));
        return;
    }
    scratch: [20]u8 = ---;
    n := 0;
    x := v;
    while x > 0 {
        scratch[n] = cast(u8, cast(s64, x % 10) + ZERO_DIGIT);
        x = x / 10;
        n = n + 1;
    }
    while n > 0 {
        n = n - 1;
        out_byte(scratch[n]);
    }
}

/// Appends a signed integer in decimal, **including the most negative one**.
///
/// `print_int` traps on `S64_MIN`, because it negates and ADR-0002 makes overflow a trap. Its own docs
/// admitted that and said the fix "needs either an unsigned path or `-%`". It needs both, and this is
/// them: the value is *reinterpreted* as a `u64` and then wrapping-negated, which yields the magnitude
/// for every negative input — `S64_MIN` reinterprets to 2^63 and negates to 2^63, which is exactly its
/// magnitude. No value is out of range, because a `u64` holds every `s64`'s magnitude.
out_s64 :: (v: s64) {
    if v < 0 {
        out_byte(cast(u8, MINUS));
        zero: u64 = 0;
        out_u64(zero -% cast(u64, v));
        return;
    }
    out_u64(cast(u64, v));
}

/// Appends `0x` and the value in lower-case hexadecimal, with no leading zeros.
out_hex :: (v: u64) {
    out_str("0x");
    if v == 0 {
        out_byte(cast(u8, ZERO_DIGIT));
        return;
    }
    scratch: [16]u8 = ---;
    n := 0;
    x := v;
    while x > 0 {
        digit := cast(s64, x % 16);
        if digit < 10 {
            scratch[n] = cast(u8, digit + ZERO_DIGIT);
        } else {
            scratch[n] = cast(u8, digit - 10 + LOWER_A);
        }
        x = x / 16;
        n = n + 1;
    }
    while n > 0 {
        n = n - 1;
        out_byte(scratch[n]);
    }
}

/// How many fraction digits a float is rendered with.
///
/// Nine, which is enough to distinguish every `float32` and to show a `float64` usefully, and few enough
/// that the single rounding below cannot produce a wrong leading digit.
FRACTION_DIGITS :: 9;

/// Ten to the power of [`FRACTION_DIGITS`].
FRACTION_SCALE :: 1000000000;

/// The magnitude above which a float is rendered in scientific notation.
///
/// 1e17 is below `u64`'s range, so the integer part below is always exact when this branch is not taken.
SCIENTIFIC_ABOVE :: 1.0e17;

/// Appends a float in decimal (ADR-0189 §5).
///
/// # What this is, and what it is not
///
/// It renders the sign, the integer part exactly, and **nine rounded fraction digits** with trailing
/// zeros trimmed. It is *not* shortest-round-trip: `0.1` prints as `0.1`, but a value whose shortest
/// representation needs more than nine fraction digits prints rounded, so reading the output back does
/// not always reproduce the input bit for bit.
///
/// That is a deliberate, documented limit rather than an oversight. Shortest-round-trip needs Ryū or
/// Grisu — a table-driven algorithm of a few hundred lines, which `docs/jai-parity.md` ranks as the third
/// most valuable thing this library lacks precisely because `modules/JSON` defers serialisation for it.
/// Doing it here badly would put a second, worse float renderer in the library and make the good one
/// harder to adopt.
///
/// The integer part is exact for every `|x| < 1e17`, because it is taken through `u64`. Above that, and
/// for a subnormal, the value goes to scientific notation, where the mantissa gets the same treatment.
out_float :: (x: float64) {
    // NaN first, because it fails every comparison below — including `x < 0.0` — so any other order
    // would print it as a number. `x != x` is the only test that identifies it without a library.
    if x != x {
        out_str("NaN");
        return;
    }
    v := x;
    if v < 0.0 {
        out_byte(cast(u8, MINUS));
        v = 0.0 - v;
    }
    if v > 1.0e308 {
        out_str("Infinity");
        return;
    }
    if v >= SCIENTIFIC_ABOVE {
        out_scientific(v);
        return;
    }
    // The integer part, exact: `v < 1e17` fits a `u64` with room to spare, and a float-to-int cast
    // truncates toward zero.
    whole := cast(u64, v);
    out_u64(whole);
    fraction := v - cast(float64, whole);
    // One multiply and one rounding, then the digits are read out of an integer — so the digits emitted
    // are exactly those of the rounded scaled value, with no per-digit error accumulation.
    scaled := cast(u64, fraction * cast(float64, FRACTION_SCALE) + 0.5);
    if scaled >= cast(u64, FRACTION_SCALE) {
        // The rounding carried into the integer part. Re-emit is not possible — the whole part is
        // already written — so the carry is dropped and the fraction clamps to all nines. This is the
        // one place the renderer is knowingly off by one in the last digit, and it needs the exact
        // algorithm to fix rather than a bigger hammer.
        scaled = cast(u64, FRACTION_SCALE) - 1;
    }
    if scaled == 0 {
        return;
    }
    out_byte(cast(u8, DOT));
    out_fraction(scaled);
}

/// Appends `scaled` as [`FRACTION_DIGITS`] digits with leading zeros kept and trailing zeros trimmed.
out_fraction :: (scaled: u64) {
    digits: [FRACTION_DIGITS]u8 = ---;
    x := scaled;
    i := FRACTION_DIGITS;
    while i > 0 {
        i = i - 1;
        digits[i] = cast(u8, cast(s64, x % 10) + ZERO_DIGIT);
        x = x / 10;
    }
    // Trailing zeros carry no information about the value, and `1.5` reads better than `1.500000000`.
    last := FRACTION_DIGITS;
    while last > 1 && digits[last - 1] == cast(u8, ZERO_DIGIT) {
        last = last - 1;
    }
    i = 0;
    while i < last {
        out_byte(digits[i]);
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

/// Appends a large positive float as `d.dddddddddde+NN`.
///
/// Reached only for `|x| >= 1e17`, where the integer part no longer fits a `u64` exactly. The mantissa is
/// brought into `[1, 10)` by repeated division, which costs one rounding per step — so this is the least
/// exact path in the renderer and is documented as such above.
out_scientific :: (v: float64) {
    mantissa := v;
    exponent := 0;
    while mantissa >= 10.0 {
        mantissa = mantissa / 10.0;
        exponent = exponent + 1;
    }
    whole := cast(u64, mantissa);
    out_u64(whole);
    fraction := mantissa - cast(float64, whole);
    scaled := cast(u64, fraction * cast(float64, FRACTION_SCALE) + 0.5);
    if scaled > 0 && scaled < cast(u64, FRACTION_SCALE) {
        out_byte(cast(u8, DOT));
        out_fraction(scaled);
    }
    out_str("e+");
    out_u64(cast(u64, exponent));
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Reading a value back out of an `Any`
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Reads a signed integer of `size` bytes, sign-extended to `s64`.
///
/// A width switch rather than a memcpy, because a narrow signed value must be **sign-extended** and only
/// a load at its own type does that: reading an `s8` of -1 as a byte gives 255.
read_signed :: (p: *u8, size: s64) -> s64 {
    if size == 1 {
        return cast(s64, typed(s8, p).*);
    }
    if size == 2 {
        return cast(s64, typed(s16, p).*);
    }
    if size == 4 {
        return cast(s64, typed(s32, p).*);
    }
    return typed(s64, p).*;
}

/// Reads an unsigned integer of `size` bytes, zero-extended to `u64`.
read_unsigned :: (p: *u8, size: s64) -> u64 {
    if size == 1 {
        return cast(u64, p.*);
    }
    if size == 2 {
        return cast(u64, typed(u16, p).*);
    }
    if size == 4 {
        return cast(u64, typed(u32, p).*);
    }
    return typed(u64, p).*;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Dispatch
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Appends one value, formatted by its own type (ADR-0189 §4).
///
/// Dispatches on `Type_Info_Kind`, which is what makes `%` need no type letter. An `if` chain rather than
/// a `switch`, deliberately: a `switch` over this enum must be exhaustive (ADR-0067), so every kind this
/// procedure treats identically would need its own arm, and the *default* below is the honest answer for
/// several of them at once.
format_any :: (a: Any) {
    kind := a.type.kind;
    if kind == Type_Info_Kind.INTEGER {
        if a.type.signed {
            out_s64(read_signed(a.data, a.type.size));
        } else {
            out_u64(read_unsigned(a.data, a.type.size));
        }
        return;
    }
    if kind == Type_Info_Kind.BOOL {
        if a.data.* != 0 {
            out_str("true");
        } else {
            out_str("false");
        }
        return;
    }
    if kind == Type_Info_Kind.STRING {
        out_str(typed(string, a.data).*);
        return;
    }
    if kind == Type_Info_Kind.FLOAT {
        // `float32` is widened rather than rendered separately: the digits come from a `float64`
        // computation either way, and a separate path would be a second renderer to keep in step.
        if a.type.size == 4 {
            out_float(cast(float64, typed(float32, a.data).*));
        } else {
            out_float(typed(float64, a.data).*);
        }
        return;
    }
    if kind == Type_Info_Kind.POINTER {
        out_hex(read_unsigned(a.data, a.type.size));
        return;
    }
    if kind == Type_Info_Kind.ENUM {
        // The **numeric** value. An enum's member names are not in `Type_Info` — `fields` describes a
        // struct's members and `count` is 0 for an enum — so printing `Colour.RED` needs a member table
        // the compiler does not emit. Recorded in ADR-0189 §6 as owed rather than faked.
        out_s64(read_signed(a.data, a.type.size));
        return;
    }
    if kind == Type_Info_Kind.STRUCT || kind == Type_Info_Kind.UNION || kind == Type_Info_Kind.VARIANT {
        format_aggregate(a);
        return;
    }
    if kind == Type_Info_Kind.ARRAY {
        format_array(a);
        return;
    }
    // Everything left — a view, a dynamic array, a procedure, `void`. Its type name is the most a caller
    // can be told, and for a structural type that name is the lowercased *kind* rather than a spelling
    // (`<view>`, not `<[]s64>`), because the compiler records no name for one. ADR-0189 §6 records both
    // that and the deeper blocker.
    //
    // **A view is not here for a different reason than a procedure is**, and the difference is worth
    // knowing: a view's elements are reachable — its header holds `data` and `count` — but its *stride*
    // is not, because `Type_Info.element` is an id and an id yields no size. An array escapes that only
    // because its own `size` divided by its `count` is the stride.
    out_byte(cast(u8, 60));
    out_str(a.type.name);
    out_byte(cast(u8, 62));
}

/// Appends a fixed-size array as `[a, b, c]`.
///
/// The stride is `size / count` rather than the element type's own size, which is unreachable: `element`
/// is a type **id**, and an id answers identity questions only (ADR-0077 §1). Dividing works because a
/// fixed array's `size` *is* `count` strides by definition — there is no trailing padding to mislead it.
///
/// Elements go through [`format_field`], the same id-comparison ladder a struct's fields use, so an array
/// of any builtin prints its values and an array of anything else prints `…` per element. One ladder for
/// both, rather than a second copy that could disagree about what a `u16` looks like.
format_array :: (a: Any) {
    out_byte(cast(u8, 91));
    count := a.type.count;
    if count > 0 {
        stride := a.type.size / count;
        i := 0;
        while i < count {
            if i > 0 {
                out_str(", ");
            }
            format_field(a.data + i * stride, a.type.element);
            i = i + 1;
        }
    }
    out_byte(cast(u8, 93));
}

/// Appends a struct, union or variant as `{name = value, …}`.
///
/// One level deep, and the limit is a real one rather than a choice: `Type_Info_Field.ty` is a type
/// **id**, not a `*Type_Info`, so a field's *kind* cannot be looked up — only compared against an id the
/// caller already holds. So every scalar field is matched against the builtin ids below, and a field of
/// any other type prints as `…`. ADR-0189 §6 records what would lift this.
format_aggregate :: (a: Any) {
    out_byte(cast(u8, 123));
    fields := a.type.fields;
    i := 0;
    while i < fields.count {
        if i > 0 {
            out_str(", ");
        }
        out_str(fields[i].name);
        out_str(" = ");
        format_field(a.data + fields[i].offset, fields[i].ty);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    out_byte(cast(u8, 125));
}

/// Appends one field, identified by comparing its type id against every builtin's.
///
/// Thirteen comparisons rather than a lookup, because a lookup needs a runtime type registry. The ids
/// come from `type_info(T)`, which the compiler folds to a constant, so each comparison is against a
/// constant and none of this costs a call.
format_field :: (p: *u8, ty: s64) {
    if ty == type_info(s64).id {
        out_s64(typed(s64, p).*);
        return;
    }
    if ty == type_info(s32).id {
        out_s64(cast(s64, typed(s32, p).*));
        return;
    }
    if ty == type_info(s16).id {
        out_s64(cast(s64, typed(s16, p).*));
        return;
    }
    if ty == type_info(s8).id {
        out_s64(cast(s64, typed(s8, p).*));
        return;
    }
    if ty == type_info(u64).id {
        out_u64(typed(u64, p).*);
        return;
    }
    if ty == type_info(u32).id {
        out_u64(cast(u64, typed(u32, p).*));
        return;
    }
    if ty == type_info(u16).id {
        out_u64(cast(u64, typed(u16, p).*));
        return;
    }
    if ty == type_info(u8).id {
        out_u64(cast(u64, p.*));
        return;
    }
    if ty == type_info(float64).id {
        out_float(typed(float64, p).*);
        return;
    }
    if ty == type_info(float32).id {
        out_float(cast(float64, typed(float32, p).*));
        return;
    }
    if ty == type_info(bool).id {
        if p.* != 0 {
            out_str("true");
        } else {
            out_str("false");
        }
        return;
    }
    if ty == type_info(string).id {
        out_str(typed(string, p).*);
        return;
    }
    // A nested aggregate, a pointer, an array. Not describable without a `*Type_Info` for `ty`.
    out_str("..");
}

/// Walks `fmt`, emitting literal bytes and substituting each `%`.
format_into_out :: (fmt: string, args: []Any) {
    i := 0;
    next := 0;
    while i < fmt.count {
        c := (fmt.data + i).*;
        if c != cast(u8, PERCENT) {
            out_byte(c);
            i = i + 1;
            continue;
        }
        // `%%` is a literal `%`, and it is checked before the argument path so a doubled percent never
        // consumes one.
        if i + 1 < fmt.count && (fmt.data + i + 1).* == cast(u8, PERCENT) {
            out_byte(cast(u8, PERCENT));
            i = i + 2;
            continue;
        }
        if next < args.count {
            format_any(args[next]);
            next = next + 1;
        } else {
            out_str("%!(MISSING)");
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    // Extra arguments are reported in the output rather than dropped. A dropped argument is a bug that
    // leaves no trace, and this is a procedure with nowhere to return an error to.
    if next < args.count {
        out_str("%!(EXTRA ");
        // Compared against the **first extra's** index, not against zero. `next > 0` was true for the
        // very first extra whenever the format string consumed any argument at all, so a one-`%` format
        // with two spare arguments printed a leading comma.
        first := next;
        while next < args.count {
            if next > first {
                out_str(", ");
            }
            format_any(args[next]);
            next = next + 1;
        }
        out_byte(cast(u8, 41));
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Module-private helpers (ADR-0054 §1)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This section held the two helpers `print_int` needed before it had a buffer: `put_byte`, which wrote one
// byte through the address of a local because `write` wants a `*u8` and a count, and `print_digits`, the
// recursion that emitted digits high-to-low without anywhere to put them. **Both are gone** (ADR-0189 §4).
//
// They are deleted rather than left unused, and that is the interesting part. `print_int` now delegates to
// the formatter, so keeping them would leave a second route to decimal digits beside the real one — and a
// second route is a second chance to disagree, which these two demonstrably did: they trapped on `S64_MIN`
// while the formatter renders it, because they negated and it does not.
//
// The formatter's own helpers stay above this line, so the dogfooding ADR-0054 asks for is unchanged: the
// standard library's internals are still the first thing hidden by `#scope_module`.
#scope_export

/// Writes a number in decimal.
///
/// # What this used to be, and why it is one line now
///
/// It handled the sign itself and drove a recursion, one syscall per digit, and it **trapped on
/// `S64_MIN`**: `-(-9223372036854775808)` overflows and ADR-0002 makes that a trap rather than a wrap, so
/// the one integer this library could not print was the one a reader is most likely to reach for when
/// testing an integer printer. Its own docs said so and named "an unsigned path or `-%`" as the fix.
///
/// Both arrived with the formatter (ADR-0189 §4), which renders through an unsigned magnitude and so has
/// no negation to overflow. Delegating rather than keeping a second renderer is the point: two paths to
/// decimal digits are two chances to disagree about `S64_MIN`, and this one was the wrong one.
///
/// Kept as a name because programs and the corpus call it, and because `print("%", n)` needs a format
/// string where this needs nothing.
print_int :: (n: s64) {
    print("%", n);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Integer printing: what it took, and what it owed
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Kept because the arc is the useful part, and both entries are now closed.
//
// This section first said `print_int` was **not implementable** — PLAN §1.2 discovered that while the
// slice was being built, and it was right: turning a digit into a byte for `write` needs an `s64` to `u8`
// conversion, and `cast` (ADR-0037) was a wave away.
//
// It then listed two things still owed, and named them precisely enough to be checked:
//
//   * **A buffer.** `print_digits` recursed one frame per digit, because emitting digits high-end-first
//     needs either a buffer or a stack and only one existed. Closed by ADR-0189: the formatter writes into
//     a file-scope `[..]u8` (ADR-0186 made a file-scope variable possible) and reaches `write` once per
//     call instead of once per digit.
//   * **The most negative `s64`.** `print_int` negated a negative, and negating -9223372036854775808
//     overflows, which ADR-0002 makes a trap. Closed by the same wave, by the route this note guessed:
//     the formatter renders through an unsigned magnitude, so there is no negation to overflow.
//
// Neither was faked while it was owed, and that is why both could be closed cleanly rather than found to
// have been quietly wrong: a wrong digit would have been worse than a missing feature, and a trap that
// says so is checkable where a wrong digit is not.
